# zoom75-screen

Drive the screen on a Meletrix / Wuque Studio **Zoom75** keyboard from Linux —
custom images and animations, a system dashboard, the built-in CPU / GPU / fan /
network / weather screens, the module clock, notes, and firmware inspection.

The vendors ship a phone app (PocketWuque, Bluetooth) and a Windows app
(MeletrixID, USB) and nothing for Linux. This replaces both.

There are **two independent channels**, and knowing which is which explains most
of the behaviour here:

| | |
|---|---|
| **BLE** | The screen module is its own peripheral, advertising as `Zoom75 Screen` even while the keyboard is on USB or 2.4 GHz. Carries custom images, animations, the module clock and notes. |
| **USB raw HID** | Feeds the *built-in* CPU / GPU / fan / network / weather screens. These read 0 unless something is pushing to them — that is what the daemon is for. |

```
./z75 scan                      # find it
./z75 info                      # status, battery, firmware
./z75 image photo.png           # upload a still
./z75 gif loop.gif --speed 7    # upload an animation
./z75 text "build passing"      # render text and upload
./z75 time                      # set the module's own clock
./z75 restore                   # back to the built-in dial
./z75 note "Deploy" "v2.1 green"  # store a note (viewable on the device)
./z75 notify "title" "body"     # push a notification (acked; see caveats)
./z75 usage / alarms / find     # queries this hardware acks but does not answer
./z75 backlight <level> <timeout>
./z75 danger poweroff|testmode|unbind   # undocumented, gated, never fired here
```

## Install

```
uv venv --python 3.13 .venv
uv pip install --python .venv/bin/python bleak pillow
./z75 --help
```

## Panel

| | |
|---|---|
| Resolution | 320 x 172 |
| Pixel format | RGB565, big-endian, row-major |
| Frame size | 110,080 bytes |

Images are scaled to fill by default (`--fit cover`); `contain` letterboxes and
`stretch` ignores the aspect ratio.

## Protocol

Reverse-engineered from the vendor Android app *PocketWuque*
(`com.app.smartkeyboard` v1.1.3) — see `PROTOCOL.md` for the full write-up.

### GATT

Service `1f40eaf8-aab4-14a3-f1ba-f61f35cddbaa`

| Characteristic | Use |
|---|---|
| `1f400001-…` | command writes |
| `1f400002-…` | replies (notify) |
| `1f400003-…` | bulk image data |
| `1f400004-…` | bulk status (notify) |

### Framing

```
88 00 00 LL LL LL LL XX  <payload>
         └─ len, BE u32  └─ XOR of payload
```

The payload opens with a 2-byte opcode; the screen replies with `opcode + 1`
(`0x0013` → `0x0014`). The device does **not** validate the XOR byte — the
vendor app hard-codes it to `00` in two places.

### Upload sequence

1. `0x0903` — announce size and still/animated. Reply `0x0904`: `4` ok,
   `1` rejected, `5` busy.
2. `0x0803` — begin. Reply `0x0804` status `2`.
3. Poll `0x0013` until the status byte reads `3` (armed).
4. Stream the pixels to `1f400003` in 244-byte packets (243 payload + XOR),
   grouped into 4096-byte blocks. The first packet carries a 25-byte header.
   The screen reports progress and failures on `0x0806`.

Requires a negotiated ATT MTU of 247. BlueZ only reveals the real MTU once a
write socket is acquired, which the client does on connect.

## Stats dashboard

`z75 stats` renders CPU / GPU / memory load bars, temperatures and live network
throughput, then uploads it.

```
./z75 stats                          # one shot
./z75 stats --watch --interval 60    # keep refreshing
./z75 stats --dry-run --save d.png   # preview without touching the keyboard
./z75 stats --iface wlp6s0           # pick a specific interface
```

`--watch` holds the BLE connection open between refreshes, so each update costs
only the upload rather than a fresh scan and connect. It also reconnects by
itself if the link drops, so it is safe to leave running. While it is connected
it re-syncs the module clock every `--sync-every` seconds (6h default) for free.

**There is deliberately no clock on the dashboard.** A frame takes ~35s to
upload and the panel only redraws once a minute, so a rendered clock was always
showing a time that had already passed — 35 to 95 seconds stale, drifting in
and out of phase with the minute. Anything displayed here has to tolerate that
lag, which load bars and throughput do and a clock does not.

The header shows the hostname and the machine's IPv4 address, read from the
interface via `SIOCGIFADDR` — not the connect-to-a-public-IP trick, so it asks
about the exact interface whose throughput is on screen and touches the network
not at all. It falls back to the interface name if the link has no address.

Sources: `/proc/stat` (CPU), `k10temp`/`coretemp` via hwmon (CPU temp),
`nvidia-smi` (GPU load, VRAM, temp, power), `/proc/meminfo`, and
`/sys/class/net/<iface>/statistics` for throughput. The GPU row is dropped
automatically if `nvidia-smi` is absent, and the interface defaults to whichever
one holds the default route.

Use `--dry-run --save` when adjusting the layout — it renders in milliseconds
instead of costing a 35-second upload per iteration.

To keep it running, a user unit:

```ini
# ~/.config/systemd/user/zoom75-stats.service
[Unit]
Description=Zoom75 screen stats dashboard
After=bluetooth.target

[Service]
ExecStart=%h/Projects/zoom75-screen/z75 stats --watch --interval 60
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=15

[Install]
WantedBy=default.target
```

## Time sync

```
./z75 time                              # set the module clock from this machine
./z75 time --read                       # read it back
./z75 time --watch --interval 21600     # re-sync every 6 hours
```

The read-back goes through an **undocumented second BLE command space** the
vendor app never uses — see "A second BLE protocol" in `PROTOCOL.md`. The
documented `0x88` space has no way to read the clock.

The module has a real-time clock, set with opcode `0x0401` (`YYYY` big-endian,
month, day, hour, minute, second). The app never sends it alone — every connect
is a time sync immediately followed by the fixed `0x0402` device-info command —
so `sync_time()` sends both and checks for the `0x0702` ack on each. There is no
read-back command: `0x0002` only acks, so that ack is the only confirmation
available.

`--watch` disconnects between syncs on purpose. The screen stops advertising
while connected, so holding the link open for hours to make one small write
every six hours would lock out every other command.

As a user unit plus timer:

```ini
# ~/.config/systemd/user/zoom75-time.service
[Unit]
Description=Sync the Zoom75 screen module clock
After=bluetooth.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=%h/Projects/zoom75-screen/z75 time
```

```ini
# ~/.config/systemd/user/zoom75-time.timer
[Unit]
Description=Sync the Zoom75 screen module clock periodically

[Timer]
OnBootSec=2min
OnUnitActiveSec=6h
Persistent=true

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
```

If you are already running `z75 stats --watch`, you do not need this — it syncs
on the connection it already holds.

### Verifying it

The module has a real clock screen and it does reflect what `z75 time` sets —
confirmed on hardware.

Sending `0x0401` on its own did **not** appear to take; sending it followed by
`0x0402`, the way the app does on every connect, did. That pairing is why
`sync_time()` always sends both rather than just the time.

The clock screen is selected on the keyboard itself, not over BLE. The `0x040D`
style command is only ever sent by the app's `second` package (the 2nd-gen
390x390 module), so `z75 style` is not the way to reach it on generation-1
hardware — and note that uploading a custom dial replaces what the panel shows,
so `z75 restore` first if you want the built-in screens back.

Beyond the display, the RTC also timestamps notebook entries, drives alarms, and
backs the per-day usage statistics (`0x0023`).

## Speed

The panel accepts data at roughly **3.3 KB/s** and that is a hardware limit,
not a tuning problem — it needs ~70 ms to commit each 243-byte chunk, so
write-without-response only corrupts the image rather than speeding it up.

| | |
|---|---|
| Still image (110 KB) | ~35 s |
| Each extra animation frame | ~35 s |
| Connect + handshake | ~7 s |

Animations are thinned to 9 frames by default (what the vendor app does for
this keyboard); raise it with `--max-frames` if you are willing to wait.

## Firmware

```
./z75 fw check                  # installed version + ask the vendor API (read-only)
./z75 fw info <file>            # parse an image offline, no device
./z75 fw download               # fetch the newest published image
./z75 fw probe [<file>]         # read-only OTA query; with a file, dry-run the plan
./z75 fw flash <file>           # writes flash -- read the warnings first
```

`check`, `info` and `download` never touch the OTA service. `probe` issues only
the two read-only OTA queries. Only `flash` writes anything.

**On this keyboard there is currently nothing to install** — the device reports
V0.1.79 (code 335) and that is the newest firmware the vendor publishes, so
`fw check` reports no update and `fw flash` refuses the same-version image.

The read-only probe has been run against the hardware: it is the 8010H variant
(nvds `0x11`), it nominates `0x32000` as the write destination, and the plan for
the shipped image validates cleanly. Nothing has ever been erased or written.

### Why flashing is different from everything else here

A bad image upload shows garbage and you re-run it. Firmware is a raw flash
programmer: page erase, write, commit. The design is dual-bank — the device
reports which bank it is running, the host writes to the other one, and the
bootloader validates the CRC before switching — so an interrupted *transfer* is
recoverable. A bad *commit* is not, and there is no recovery path over BLE.

`ota.py` therefore refuses to proceed unless all of the following hold, and the
cheap checks all run before the OTA service is contacted at all:

- `--i-understand` was passed, and `FLASH` is typed at an interactive prompt
- the image parses, is word-aligned, and has a plausible version header
- it is newer than what is installed (`--allow-same` / `--allow-downgrade` to override)
- battery is at least 50% (`--ignore-battery` to override)
- the computed target is page-aligned and is **not** the bank the device is running
- on the fixed-bank variant, the image fits in the gap between banks
- after the last write, the device's reported address matches what was sent —
  otherwise it aborts *without* committing, leaving the running firmware untouched

`CHIP_ERASE` is deliberately never emitted.

## Built-in system screens (CPU / GPU / fan / network / weather)

These screens are fed over **USB raw HID, not BLE** — which is why they read 0
with only the Bluetooth tooling running. This is not a limitation of the
tooling: the two transports carry different command spaces, and sending the BLE
weather command to generation-1 hardware is acked and then ignored. See "The two
command spaces do not overlap" in `PROTOCOL.md`. The vendor's Windows app (MeletrixID,
which wraps LibreHardwareMonitor) pushes 32-byte reports to the keyboard's
vendor HID interface. `zoom75/hid.py` is a port of that protocol.

```
./z75 hid probe                  # show the device and the values that would go out
./z75 hid feed                   # push CPU temp / GPU temp / fan RPM / network once
./z75 hid feed --watch           # keep pushing (default every 2s)
./z75 hid weather                # fetch from Open-Meteo and push (no API key)
./z75 hid weather --code 6 --temp 12
./z75 hid time                   # push the clock over USB
```

Note the CPU and GPU screens show **temperatures**, not load — the vendor app
reads LibreHardwareMonitor's temperature sensors. Fan RPM comes from whatever
hwmon exposes; on a liquid-cooled machine that may be the AIO pump, and
`hid probe` prints which sensor it picked.

### Permissions

The hidraw nodes are root-only, and that is worth keeping. The keyboard exposes
three of them and **interface 00 is the boot keyboard** — anything that can read
it can read everything you type. The recommended answer is the service below,
which runs as root and needs no rule at all, so those nodes stay
`crw------- root root`.

If you specifically want the `z75 hid` CLI to work without sudo, there is a
narrowed rule in `udev/99-zoom75.rules` that matches **only** interface 01, the
vendor interface:

```
sudo install -m 0644 udev/99-zoom75.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/99-zoom75.rules
sudo udevadm control --reload-rules && sudo udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=hidraw
ls -l /dev/hidraw*     # the keyboard nodes must stay crw------- root root
```

Read it before installing it, and check that last command afterwards. The
service does not need this rule and its installer removes it if present.

### Protocol

32-byte reports on the vendor usage page `0xFF60`, VID `1ea7`, PID `ced3`/`cd68`:

```
[0]      0x1C
[1]      outer type: 0 sensor, 3 clock
[5]      outer length: 8 sensor, 9 weather, 15 clock
[6..7]   CRC-16/CCITT-FALSE, low byte first
[8]      0xA5
[9]      field: 37 CPU, 38 GPU, 39 fan, 3B weather, 3D network (38+type 3 = clock)
[10..11] data length, big-endian
[12..]   data, first byte always zero
[12+len] checksum: (sum(report) & 0xFF ^ 0xFF) % 255
```

Temperatures in the weather report carry their sign in bit 15 rather than as
two's complement. The weather report writes a literal `0xFF` where the checksum
belongs instead of computing one; that quirk is reproduced.

## Running it as a service

```
sudo ./install-service.sh          # install and start
sudo ./install-service.sh --uninstall
```

One daemon drives everything: CPU/GPU temperature, fan RPM and network over
USB, optionally weather and the clock, and optionally the BLE dashboard.

**Security model**

- Code installs to `/opt/zoom75-screen`, owned `root:root`. It runs as root, so
  a user-writable copy would be a privilege escalation — the installer refuses
  world-writable sources and re-chowns everything.
- Config is `/etc/zoom75/config.toml`, root-owned. **Changing what the screen
  shows requires sudo**, which is the point.
- **No udev rule.** The daemon opens the hidraw node as root; the keyboard's
  input interfaces stay unreadable to unprivileged processes. The installer
  removes the old rule if an earlier version left one behind.
- The unit is sandboxed: every capability dropped (`CapabilityBoundingSet=`),
  `ProtectSystem=strict`, `ProtectHome=yes`, `/dev` restricted to
  `char-hidraw`, `SystemCallFilter=@system-service` minus the privileged sets,
  `MemoryDenyWriteExecute`, `NoNewPrivileges`, and egress limited to what the
  weather lookup needs.

It runs as root because that is what opening `/dev/hidraw*` requires while
leaving the input interfaces closed. Everything else is dropped.

```
systemctl status zoom75-screen
journalctl -u zoom75-screen -f
sudoedit /etc/zoom75/config.toml && sudo systemctl restart zoom75-screen
```

BLE is off by default in the config: an upload takes ~35s and holds the link,
which blocks the `z75` CLI for that whole time.

## Coverage

Every command reachable in the vendor app is implemented; `PROTOCOL.md` has the
full opcode table with per-command status. Summary for generation-1 hardware:

- **Verified working**: dial upload (still + animated), restore, device version,
  status, clock sync, notes (write, timestamp read, delete), firmware probe.
- **Acked but inert here**: notifications (`0x0501`), usage stats (`0x0023`),
  alarms (`0x0025`), system data (`0x0017`), find device (`0x0019`), style
  (`0x040D`). The device recognises each opcode — it echoes it in the ack — but
  returns no data and shows nothing. Several are 2nd-generation features.
- **Implemented, deliberately never fired**: backlight, select local dial,
  unbind, set current face, power off, test mode. The last three carry the SDK's
  `A1 FE 74 69` magic tail and sit behind `z75 danger` plus `--i-understand`.

## Caveats

- The advertised address is a non-resolvable private address and can change, so
  the tool matches on the service UUID / name rather than caching a MAC.
- The screen stops advertising while connected. If a run is killed mid-transfer
  the link can leak and `scan` will find nothing — `bluetoothctl disconnect
  <addr>` clears it. The CLI handles SIGTERM so `timeout` won't leave one behind.
- An interrupted upload can make the *next* one fail with a GATT error partway.
  Disconnect and re-run; the fresh handshake recovers it.
- `./z75 restore` returns the screen to its built-in dial if an upload leaves it
  showing something you don't want.

## Status

Both the still and animated paths are confirmed working on real hardware. See
`tests.py` for the encoder checks (run `.venv/bin/python tests.py`), which
verify the frame builders against byte strings lifted from the vendor app.


## Credits and status

Unofficial and unaffiliated with Meletrix or Wuque Studio. Nothing here is
endorsed or supported by them.

The protocols were reverse-engineered for interoperability from the vendors' own
clients — PocketWuque (`com.app.smartkeyboard`) for Bluetooth and MeletrixID for
USB. `PROTOCOL.md` documents what was found, including a couple of bugs in the
originals. No vendor code or firmware is redistributed in this repository;
`z75 fw download` fetches images from Meletrix's own server.

Developed against a generation-1 Zoom75 (product `c003`, firmware V0.1.79).
Behaviour on the 2nd-generation 390x390 module, Tiga, Dyna or Hetix is
untested — several commands are marked in `PROTOCOL.md` as belonging to those
models.

**Firmware flashing is implemented but has never been executed.** Read the
warnings in `PROTOCOL.md` before using `z75 fw flash`.

